[38;5;231m⏺[39m One consequence worth stating plainly, because it cuts against why "in-app only" looked cheap: [1mmulti-user [0m
  [1mnotifications need a server and a database regardless.[0m If someone else changes a task and you're supposed to find out,
  that change has to live somewhere both browsers can see. Browser-local storage can't do it. What you saved by
  skipping email was the mail provider, the job runner, and unsubscribe compliance — not the backend.

  So there's a real stack decision. It shapes every later plan, and it's the single most expensive thing to change your
  mind about.
